course: dictionary
lesson: 118
title: Adorable and the drop-e rule
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Adorable is an adjective, so it describes a noun, like «an adorable puppy». It means very sweet, cute and lovely, so that you want to cuddle it or look after it.
explain: People use it for babies, small animals and cute things. It can also mean charming and easy to love, as when a funny character wins everyone over. Look at the words around it: «absolutely adorable» and «so adorable» show that the writer really likes the thing.
explain: Adorable comes from the verb adore, which means to love very much. Before -able, the final e of the root usually drops, so adore becomes adorable. Check the spelling carefully when you write it.
fact: Adorable means very sweet, cute and lovely.
fact: «An adorable puppy» is a puppy that makes you want to cuddle it.
fact: Adorable is stronger than cute: «absolutely adorable» and «so adorable» show a bigger feeling.
fact: Adorable can also mean charming and easy to love.
fact: Before -able, a final e usually drops (adore, adorable), but it stays after a soft c or g (notice, noticeable; change, changeable).
exercise: «The adorable puppy wagged its tail.» What does adorable mean here? | Very angry and loud | Very tall and thin | Dirty and wet from the rain | *Very sweet and lovely, so you want to cuddle it
exercise: Adore plus -able makes which spelling? | adoreable | adorrable | *adorable
exercise: The new girl was charming and easy to love, and the whole class liked her. Which word from this lesson means the same? | Treacherous | Famished | *Adorable | Curt
test: Why does changeable keep its e before -able? | Because changeable is a verb | Because the e is always kept before -able | *Because the g needs the e to stay soft
test: «The kitten's sleepy yawn was so adorable.» What is the best meaning of adorable? | Rude and short | Very loud and angry | *Very sweet and lovely | Lost and afraid
exam: Which spelling and reason are correct for adore plus -able? | *adorable, because the final e drops | adorable, because the word is a noun | adoreable, because the e always stays | adorrable, because the e turns into an r
